      "output": "Title: OVER YOUR HEAD definition | Cambridge English Dictionary\nURL: https://dictionary.cambridge.org/us/dictionary/english/over-head\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\ntoo difficult or strange for you to understand:\n...\ngo over your head I tried to take in what he was saying about nuclear fusion, but most of it went over my head.\n...\ntoo difficult for someone to understand:\n...\nMost of that lecture was over my head.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Definition of 'be over sb's head'\nURL: https://www.collinsdictionary.com/us/dictionary/english/be-over-someones-head\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nIf something such as an idea, joke, or comment goes over someone's head, it is too difficult for them to understand.\n...\nI admit that a lot of the ideas went way over my head.\n\n---\n\nTitle: GO OVER SOMEONE'S HEAD definition | Cambridge English Dictionary\nURL: https://dictionary.cambridge.org/us/dictionary/english/go-over-head\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nto speak to or ask permission from someone who has more authority than the person who you would normally go to in that situation:\n...\nAmanda was refusing to give me the week off so I went over her head and spoke to the boss.\n...\nto be too difficult or strange for someone to understand:\n...\nSome of what the teacher said went over the children's heads.\n\n---\n\nTitle: GO OVER SB'S HEAD definition | Cambridge Learner’s Dictionary\nURL: https://dictionary.cambridge.org/us/dictionary/learner-english/go-over-sb-s-head\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nto be too difficult for someone to understand:\n...\nAll this talk about philosophy went right over my head.\n\n---\n\nTitle: over your head | Learn English\nURL: https://www.englishclub.com/ref/esl/Idioms/O/over_your_head_173.php\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nIf something you hear or read is over your head, or goes over your head, you don't understand it because the language or the ideas are too advanced for you.\n...\n- I tried listening to a show on the radio about modern physics, but most of it was over my head and I gave up after about ten minutes.\n- Jerry told a really funny joke about the economics of the cheese industry, and it went right over Tom's head. He didn't even smile.\n...\nI could tell the lecture had gone over my student's head because he\n...\na. couldn't answer a simple question b. made intelligent comments c. asked interesting questions\n...\na) couldn't answer a simple question b) made intelligent comments c) asked interesting questions\n\n---\n\nTitle: Go over someone's head - Idioms by The Free Dictionary\nURL: https://idioms.thefreedictionary.com/go+over+someone%27s+head\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n1. To speak to one's superior rather than talking to one directly. She just wouldn't listen to me, so I had to go over her head to her boss and complain about it. This caller wants to go over my head, and I can't say I blame her—based on the notes here, she's already called seven times to try and get this issue resolved. Talking to customer service reps isn't getting you anywhere. You really need to go over their head to their manager.\n...\n2. To not be understood by one because it is too complex or subtle for their comprehension. My jokes always seem to go over Stephanie's head, so I'm glad you think I'm funny at least. A: \"My uncle is reading a book about anti-gravity technology. He says he can't put it down!\" B: \"Oh, wow, that does sound really interesting.\" A: \"Well, that went over your head, now didn't it?\" All of this legalese will go over people's heads, so please put the report into language anyone could understand.\n...\n1. If you go over the head of someone in authority, you communicate directly with someone in a higher position to try to get what you want. Don't break office protocol by going over your boss's head. He was reprimanded for trying to go over the heads of senior officers.\n...\n2. If something that someone says or writes goes over your head, you do not understand it because it is too difficult for you. A lot of what he writes goes over my head. Note: You can also say that something is over your head. Most of the article was over my head. Compare with talk over someone's head.\n\n---\n\nTitle: go over someone's head - Wiktionary, the free dictionary\nURL: https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/go_over_someone's_head\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\ngo over someone's head(third-person singular simple present goes over someone's head, present participle going over someone's head, simple past went over someone's head, past participle gone over someone's head)\n...\n(idiomatic) To take up an issue with another person's boss or other superior rather than beginning or continuing to deal with the original person.\n...\nShe went over his head and took her complaint directly to the president of the company.\n...\n(idiomatic) To escape someone's comprehension.\n...\nI listened carefully, but the technical jargon went over my head.\n\n---\n\nTitle: It went right over your head - Idioms by The Free Dictionary\nURL: https://idioms.thefreedictionary.com/it+went+right+over+your+head\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n# go over (one's) head\n...\n## go over (one's) head\n...\n1. To speak to one's superior rather than talking to one directly. She just wouldn't listen to me, so I had to go over her head to her boss and complain about it. This caller wants to go over my head, and I can't say I blame her—based on the notes here, she's already called seven times to try and get this issue resolved. Talking to customer service reps isn't getting you anywhere. You really need to go over their head to their manager.\n...\n2. To not be understood by one because it is too complex or subtle for their comprehension. My jokes always seem to go over Stephanie's head, so I'm glad you think I'm funny at least. A: \"My uncle is reading a book about anti-gravity technology. He says he can't put it down!\" B: \"Oh, wow, that does sound really interesting.\" A: \"Well, that went over your head, now didn't it?\" All of this legalese will go over people's heads, so please put the report into language anyone could understand.\n...\n## go over someone's head\n...\n1. If you go over the head of someone in authority, you communicate directly with someone in a higher position to try to get what you want. Don't break office protocol by going over your boss's head. He was reprimanded for trying to go over the heads of senior officers.\n...\n2. If something that someone says or writes goes over your head, you do not understand it because it is too difficult for you. A lot of what he writes goes over my head. Note: You can also say that something is over your head. Most of the article was over my head. Compare with talk over someone's head.\n...\n- it went right over your head\n...\n- it went on the swag\n- it went out in\n...\nblaze of glory\n- it went out of favor\n...\n- it went out of\n...\n- it went out on top\n- it went over a cliff\n- it went over her head\n- it went over his head\n- it went over like a lead balloon\n- it went over like a lead zeppelin\n- it went over my head\n- it went over one's head\n- it went over our heads\n- it went over somebody's head\n- it went over someone's head\n- it went over the cliff\n- it went over their heads\n- it went over to the dark side\n- it went over your head\n- it went overboard\n- it went past the point of no return\n- it went quietly into the night\n- it went right over her head\n- it went right over his head\n- it went right over my head\n- it went right over one's head\n- it went right over our heads\n- it went right over somebody's head\n- it went right over someone's head\n- it went right over their heads\n- it went right over your head",
